        <section class="flow has-gap-s" >
            <h2>About</h2>
            <p>Originally from Italy, I come from a family of makers, artists, and business owners. As a child I dreamt first of becoming a magician, then of designing chairs. And while neither of those dreams came true, I didn’t land far off.</p>
            <p>These days, I spend my time making complex stories a little less complex, and all the while drawing audiences closer to good products, good services and good talent. </p>
            <p>When I’m not busy solving problems, you’ll find me supporting fellow artists and volunteering with <a href="https://openartfolke.com" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open Art Folke</a>. Occasionally I write and make art myself with words and pixels.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="flow has-gap-s" >
            <h2>Philosophy</h2>
            
            <p>We live in turbulent times. There is an overabundance of information, and in the midst of this turmoil, I see designers taking on facilitator roles and helping people communicate ideas.</p>
            <p>When it comes to marketing, I specialise in the trust-based kind—or the art of building credibility and long-term relationships by being honest, helpful and consistent with your audience.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="flow has-gap-m" >
            <h2>Infrequently asked questions</h2>
            <div class="flow has-gap-s">
                <details>
                    <summary>How do you think about design?</summary>
                    <p>First of all, design is an attitude. Good design, though? That's about having a strong opinion and carrying it through.</p>
                    <p>Good design is choosing the right compromise for your audience. Good design is knowing when to bend the process in your favour. Skip steps when you deem them unnecessary. Backtrack when you're unsatisfied. Maybe start from the solution first, or operate on intuition, or make something just for the sake of making people smile.</p>
                    <p>Good design understands the power of rhythm and play, and knows how to use them. Good design is harder to spot than poor design. And it may be impossible to achieve, but good design always worth trying.
                </details>
                <hr>
                <details>
                    <summary>Why trust based marketing?</summary>
                    <p>With so many people losing trust in the messages being pushed by mainstream marketing, an approach based on trust is how you get people to choose you, because, well, they trust you. Not because you manipulated them, chased them with an axe or pushed them to their limit.</p>
                    <p>It's a vast and wild frontier that many marketers and brands have only begun to explore, and it's very much one of the pillars of my work. If you're looking for reassurance, for all one knows, it’s the approach also followed by brands like Oatly, Patagonia, and MUBI.</p>
                    <p>To borrow the <a href="https://x.com/ungatedlife/status/1584621315998486528" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">words of Rob Hardy</a>, the main guy <a href="https://forest.quest/non-coercive-marketing-primer/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">who championed the term</a>, "the big secret is that non-coercive marketing isn't actually about marketing at all, but instead about how we relate to ourselves and to others, and healing the wounds of distrust that ripple through generations."</p>
                    <p>My work in this field incorporates everything I have learnt from working in marketing (mostly content and inbound), internal communications, exhibition design, and graphic design over the last 8 years.</p>
                </details>
                <hr>
                <details>
                    <summary>What is your background?</summary>
                    <p>Before doing the work I now do, I graduated in Digital Arts from the University of Greenwich—which is where much of this all began—and I then spent six years between cultural institutions and the Film & TV industry.</p>
                    <p>I now live in Folkestone, on the Kent coast, but I was born in Italy in a land called Ciociaria.</p>
                </details>
            </div>
        </section>
        <section class="flow has-gap-s" >
            <h2>On sustainable actions</h2>
            
            <p>It took around 4.6 billion years for a cosmic swirl of gas and dust to become a planet suitable for birds and flowers and children. The last thing I want to do is devote my time to furthering its destruction. As such, in 2025, I joined Creatives for Climate, a global network of creative professionals who are putting their skills to work in the fight for the climate. I'm also a member of Good Folk, a collective that offers pro-bono support to charities with a lot on their plate. </p>
            <p>Although my practice is far from perfect, I see every project as an opportunity to shape the change I wish to see. And for the record, I will not work with clients in the fossil fuel sector, or those directly associated with this industry.</p>
        </section>
